{#
Month: a six-week grid that fills its container.

The grid itself — the weekday strip, the six equal rows, the hairline gutters
and the today/weekend/spill-in treatment of a cell — lives in
calendar/_month_shell.html.twig, which the public shared-calendar page embeds
too. What is left here is the only part that is about THIS calendar: the "new
event on this day" affordance, the chips that open an editor, and the link to
the day when a cell has more than fits. The shell's own header says why it is a
shell rather than two files that look alike.

Always six weeks, never five-or-six. The range enum asks for 42 days from the
Monday on or before the first, so the grid does not change height between
months — a calendar that reflows as you page through it is disorienting, and it
means the surrounding layout never has to reserve for the taller case.

`grid-rows-6` with `h-full` is what makes the six weeks share the height evenly
instead of each row shrinking to its busiest cell, which left a ragged block of
empty space under the grid. Both are the shell's defaults, so this passes
neither.
#}

{# The view's clock, not the reader's: the day keys are grouped in it, so asking
   a different one which day is today highlights the wrong cell for however many
   hours the two are apart. #}
{% set today = 'now'|date('Y-m-d', range.zone) %}

{% embed 'calendar/_month_shell.html.twig' with {
    dayKeys: days|keys,
    days: days,
    anchorMonth: anchor|date('n'),
    today: today,
    shellCellExpands: true,
} %}
    {% block cell_actions %}
        <button type="button"
           data-controller="ui--modal"
           data-action="click->ui--modal#open"
           data-ui--modal-src-value="{{ path('app_calendar_event_new', { start: dayKey }) }}"
           data-ui--modal-title-value="{{ 'calendar.event.new'|trans }}"
           aria-label="{{ 'calendar.event.new_on'|trans({ '%date%': dayKey|intl_date('EEEdMMM') }) }}"
           class="w-5 h-5 flex items-center justify-center rounded-md text-[10px] text-ink-faint
                  opacity-0 group-hover/cell:opacity-100 focus:opacity-100
                  hover:bg-hover transition-opacity
                  focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-accent"
        ><i class="fa-solid fa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></button>
    {% endblock %}

    {% block cell_date %}
        {# The way into a day. This grid reached one only through "N more",
           which appeared on busy days and nowhere else, and that link is gone
           along with the count it could not get right. The number itself is
           what every calendar anybody has used puts the link on. #}
        <a href="{{ path('app_calendar_view', { view: 'day', date: dayKey }) }}{{ isPane|default(false) ? '?pane=1' : '' }}"
           {% if frame|default(null) %}data-turbo-frame="{{ frame }}"{% endif %}
           aria-label="{{ 'calendar.open_day'|trans({ '%date%': dayKey|intl_date('EEEdMMM') }) }}"
           class="rounded-md hover:bg-hover transition-colors
                  focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-accent">
            {% if isToday %}
                <span class="w-5 h-5 flex items-center justify-center rounded-full
                             bg-accent text-accent-ink text-xs font-semibold tabular-nums">
                    {{ dayKey|date('j') }}
                </span>
            {% else %}
                <span class="block px-1 text-xs tabular-nums
                             {{ isOtherMonth ? 'text-ink-faint' : 'text-ink-muted' }}">
                    {{ dayKey|date('j') }}
                </span>
            {% endif %}
        </a>
    {% endblock %}

    {% block cell_body %}
        {# **Every entry is drawn and the BOX decides how many are seen.**
           This was a slice to two with a "+1 more" under it, and the report was
           the obvious one: there was room for a third. There always might be. A
           month cell's height is the pane's divided by six, a chip is one row
           or two depending on whether its entry has a time, and no number
           written here can be right for both a tall window and a short one.

           So nothing is counted. The list holds the whole day, the box clips
           it, and a mask fades the last few pixels so a cut chip reads as "this
           goes on" rather than as a rendering fault. Where it goes on to is the
           day's peek, which needs no count either, and the date, which is a
           link now — see the shell's `cell_date`. Gone with the count is "N
           more", and with it the only reason a cell ever spent a row on
           something that was not an event.

           Hovering a day enlarges the whole cell — the shell's card, see
           `shellCellExpands` there, which is where the no-movement rule is set
           out. All this list does about it is stop clipping: the card is what
           decides how far it opens, and it scrolls past the bottom of the grid.

           Titles stay clamped to one line while it is open — see the chip. A
           wider card is a longer line rather than a shorter stack, so no chip
           changes height and no chip below one moves. 18rem is where the
           widening stops, which is about thirty-five characters. #}
        <div class="min-h-0 flex-1 space-y-0.5 overflow-hidden
                    @min-[5.5rem]:group-hover/cell:overflow-visible @min-[5.5rem]:group-hover/cell:shrink-0
                    [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_bottom,black_calc(100%-0.625rem),transparent)]
                    group-hover/cell:[mask-image:none]">
            {% for cluster in dayEntries %}
                {% include 'calendar/_event_chip.html.twig' with { monthCell: true } %}
            {% endfor %}
        </div>

    {% endblock %}
{% endembed %}
